\begin{abstract}
We establish perfect electromagnetic cloaking in the time domain for finite-energy solutions of Maxwell's equations. The cloak is generated by a singular transformation that maps a punctured virtual space onto a physical configuration containing a cloaked region and induces anisotropic, spatially singular permittivity and permeability tensors. Maxwell evolution is formulated on the corresponding weighted energy spaces, where the exterior Maxwell operator admits a distinguished self-adjoint realization. We prove that this realization is unitarily equivalent to the free Maxwell operator in virtual space. Consequently, the associated unitary propagators are intertwined, and the exterior wave operators exist, are complete, and yield an identity scattering operator. Thus every incident finite-energy electromagnetic pulse evolves outside the cloaked region exactly as it would in homogeneous space, with no scattered field at any time. We further show that the singular interface dynamically decouples the exterior and interior fields and determines the admissible hidden boundary conditions required by self-adjointness. The resulting theorem provides a causal, time-domain formulation of perfect cloaking that is valid for broadband pulses and does not rely on time-harmonic or single-frequency arguments.
\end{abstract}
